--- ANSWER ---
Your diagnosis is strongly supported, but ?�per-op Traverse order??is slightly too specific: the failure proves only that the two independently produced arrays are not positionally compatible. Different traversal implementations, callbacks, filters, diagram roots, or object-loading states could all cause that?�not necessarily nondeterminism in Traverse itself.

1. Traverse ordering

There is no documented ordering contract for `Traverse for GObjects.vi`. Community reverse-engineering describes a complicated depth-first order influenced by creation order and container hierarchy, which is evidence of implementation behavior?�not an API guarantee. Therefore:

- Do not assume indices from `OpReportAll_v0` select the same objects in `OpReport_v3`-derived operations.
- Do not assume a cached index remains stable across executions, reloads, edits, or sessions.
- If index selection remains anywhere, retain the UID identity gate on every read.

Your 180/180 result demonstrates repeatability for that particular op family and run; it does not establish a supported stability guarantee. [NI Community discussion of Traverse ordering](https://forums.ni.com/t5/LabVIEW/String-reference/td-p/4430179)

Option A would probably repair the immediate family mismatch, but it remains dependent on undocumented ordering. C is architecturally better because it uses the actual identifier as the selector.

2. `UID to GObject Reference.vi`

The sources support these terminals semantically:

- input: target **VI reference**
- input: **UID** (`I32`)
- output: **GObject reference**
- error in/error out are also present in versions discussed by LAVA.

It takes a VI reference, not a Block Diagram reference. An NI engineer describes the intended construction as a static reference to the target VI followed by lookup of the object?�s fixed UID. [NI employee explanation](https://forums.ni.com/t5/LabVIEW-Idea-Exchange/Create-reference-in-a-new-vi-if-scripting-is-enabled/idc-p/4035635)

I could not find an authoritative text source that exposes the exact LabVIEW-2026 connector-label capitalization. Likely labels are `VI Ref`, `UID`, `GObject Ref`, `error in`, and `error out`, but treat those first three spellings as unverified until the manager obtains Context Help or reporter output from the installed VI.

Most importantly, do **not** design on the assumption that an absent UID cleanly returns an error or null reference. A reported failure mode is an occasional LabVIEW crash when the lookup receives a nonexistent/unverified UID. The VI also returns a duplicate reference that should be explicitly closed, and it works only in the development environment?�not the Runtime Engine. [LAVA investigation](https://lavag.org/topic/21759-does-anyone-know-how-to-convert-a-vi-server-refnum-to-the-underlying-pointer/?comment=133516&do=findComment), [NI Community invalid-UID warning](https://forums.ni.com/t5/LabVIEW/Unique-ID-for-all-front-panel-object/td-p/4151242/page/3)

The lookup is reported to find subobjects such as array scrollbars and individual control decorations, strongly suggesting it consults the VI-wide object registry rather than traversing only the top diagram. That supports nested case-diagram wires, but I found no explicit NI statement naming nested structure diagrams. A cheap safe verification is to submit one already-confirmed nested-wire UID?�not a fabricated negative UID?�and verify returned UID, class `Wire`, and diagram ownership. [NI Community subobject report](https://forums.ni.com/t5/LabVIEW/Unique-ID-for-all-front-panel-object/td-p/4151242/page/3)

3. UID scope and stability

There is no op-boundary problem provided both ops hold references to the same target VI instance. NI documents that a GObject UID:

- is unique within its VI;
- remains associated with the same object after saving;
- should be checked alongside class or label because a deleted object?�s UID may later be reused.

Thus a Wire UID obtained by `report_all` should resolve from another op against the same VI, including after ordinary save/reload. It is not globally unique across different VIs. Packaging or transformations can also complicate identity?�for example, a community report says building typedefs into a packed project library appeared to reassign their UIDs. [NI UID documentation](https://www.ni.com/docs/en-US/bundle/labview-api-ref/page/properties-and-methods/vi-server/generic/gobject/uid.html), [PPL caveat](https://forums.ni.com/t5/LabVIEW/Unique-ID-for-all-front-panel-object/td-p/4151242/page/3)

Keep the returned-object checks:

1. returned reference valid;
2. returned UID equals requested UID;
3. class is `Wire`;
4. optionally confirm owning VI identity.

That also protects against accidentally passing the op VI reference instead of `MAIN`.

4. `Terms[0]` and `LoopTunnel`

No: `LoopTunnel` does not itself discredit the element-zero convention. The documented scripting description says the first `Wire.Terminals[]` reference is the source when a source exists; remaining terminals have no defined order. For a broken wire with multiple sources, extra sources need not be grouped at the beginning. [Wire `Terminals[]` property](https://labviewwiki.org/wiki/Wire_class/Terminals%28%29_property)

A tunnel can legitimately be the source terminal for the wire segment on one side of a structure, so owner class `LoopTunnel` is not anomalous. NI also documents that an ordinary wire has one source and potentially many sinks. [NI wire semantics](https://www.ni.com/docs/en-AS/bundle/labview/page/using-wires-to-link-block-diagram-objects.html)

Nevertheless, checking `Is Source?` is a worthwhile defensive measure, particularly for broken or unusual wires:

- Property ID: `634A003`
- Short name/data name: `IsSource`
- Long name: `Is Source?`
- Type: Boolean, read-only

[Terminal `Is Source?` property](https://labviewwiki.org/wiki/Terminal_class/Is_Source%3F_property)

Recommended result: implement C, pass the target **VI reference**, avoid deliberately testing nonexistent UIDs because of the reported crash hazard, close the returned duplicate reference, and retain UID-plus-class verification. For valid, unbroken wires, `Terms[0]` is supported; scanning for `IsSource == TRUE` adds useful corruption/broken-wire detection but should not change the normal answer.
